---
import { Icon } from '@astrojs/starlight/components';
import ArcantryKnowledgeGraph from './ArcantryKnowledgeGraph.astro';
---

<section class="value" aria-labelledby="value-title">
  <div class="value-heading">
    <p>Why Arcantry</p>
    <div>
      <h2 id="value-title">Know what the project knows before another tool changes it</h2>
      <p>Arcantry turns specifications, changelogs, task queues, and agent skills into one inspectable source map. It keeps each source's meaning and ownership separate, so you can evaluate the whole project, validate one boundary, or plan one controlled change without forcing everything into a new repository shape.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="value-grid">
    <article>
      <Icon name="approve-check-circle" />
      <h3>See the full knowledge surface</h3>
      <p>One inspection finds existing and missing sources, their paths, roles, visibility, versions, and compatibility.</p>
    </article>
    <article>
      <Icon name="puzzle" />
      <h3>Adopt without a rewrite</h3>
      <p>Start with no configuration, keep control outside the repository, or track one shared contract when the team is ready.</p>
    </article>
    <article>
      <Icon name="approve-check" />
      <h3>Review intent before writes</h3>
      <p>Separate discovery, decisions, and application so teammates and automation can inspect the same change boundary first.</p>
    </article>
  </div>

  <div class="outcome-stage">
    <article class="outcome-feature">
      <span class="outcome-index">01</span>
      <Icon name="approve-check" />
      <div>
        <h3>Start with any project</h3>
        <p>An empty folder, a non-Git codebase, and a mature monorepo can all be inspected before Arcantry owns anything.</p>
      </div>
    </article>
    <div class="outcome-stack">
      <article>
        <span class="outcome-index">02</span>
        <Icon name="database" />
        <div>
          <h3>Keep meaning at the source</h3>
          <p>Specifications remain authority, changelogs remain projections, queues remain independent, and skills remain procedures.</p>
        </div>
      </article>
      <article>
        <span class="outcome-index">03</span>
        <Icon name="code-branch" />
        <div>
          <h3>Stop stale changes before they land</h3>
          <p>Plans capture the inputs they expect, so a changed source stops application and asks for a fresh decision.</p>
        </div>
      </article>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="comparison" aria-labelledby="comparison-title">
  <div class="comparison-heading">
    <p>With and without</p>
    <h2 id="comparison-title">Replace repeated guesswork with one explicit project model</h2>
  </div>
  <div class="comparison-table-shell">
    <table class="comparison-flow">
      <colgroup>
        <col class="comparison-area-column" />
        <col />
        <col />
      </colgroup>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Area</th>
          <th class="comparison-before-heading" scope="col">Without Arcantry</th>
          <th class="comparison-after-heading" scope="col">With Arcantry</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row"><span>01</span><div><Icon name="magnifier" /><h3>Discovery</h3></div></th>
          <td class="comparison-before"><p><strong>Every tool</strong> scans the project again and builds a different picture of what exists.</p></td>
          <td class="comparison-after"><p><strong>One inspection</strong> returns the same map of present, missing, and configured sources.</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row"><span>02</span><div><Icon name="database" /><h3>Responsibility</h3></div></th>
          <td class="comparison-before"><p><strong>OpenSpec</strong>, <strong>Changelog</strong>, <strong>todo.txt</strong>, and skills look like interchangeable files.</p></td>
          <td class="comparison-after"><p><strong>Each source keeps one role</strong>, owner, visibility, version, and management boundary.</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row"><span>03</span><div><Icon name="puzzle" /><h3>Adoption</h3></div></th>
          <td class="comparison-before"><p><strong>Trying one workflow</strong> can require the framework's complete structure and configuration.</p></td>
          <td class="comparison-after"><p><strong>Connect one capability</strong>, keep it private, or adopt the complete project model.</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row"><span>04</span><div><Icon name="code-branch" /><h3>Change</h3></div></th>
          <td class="comparison-before"><p><strong>A script can discover, decide, and write</strong> before anyone sees the exact effect.</p></td>
          <td class="comparison-after"><p>A <strong>reviewable plan</strong> separates the intended transition from later file writes.</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row"><span>05</span><div><Icon name="warning" /><h3>Drift</h3></div></th>
          <td class="comparison-before"><p><strong>A source changed after review</strong> can be overwritten by an older automation decision.</p></td>
          <td class="comparison-after"><p>Arcantry checks the <strong>expected inputs</strong> and stops when the project changed.</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row"><span>06</span><div><Icon name="setting" /><h3>Automation</h3></div></th>
          <td class="comparison-before"><p><strong>Tools duplicate compatibility rules</strong> and disagree about project boundaries.</p></td>
          <td class="comparison-after"><p>People and tools read the <strong>same source map</strong>, evidence, and boundaries.</p></td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="contract-map" aria-labelledby="contract-title">
  <div class="contract-heading">
    <p>Explore Arcantry</p>
    <h2 id="contract-title">Go from the project map to the decision you need</h2>
  </div>
  <div class="contract-lanes">
    <a class="local-lane" href="/arcantry/reference/repository-contract/#roles-in-the-stack">
      <div class="lane-title"><Icon name="database" /><span>Project knowledge stack</span></div>
      <h3>Understand the model before changing anything</h3>
      <p>See why authority, release projections, queues, skills, private state, and integrity evidence stay separate even when one tool can inspect them together.</p>
      <span class="lane-action">Understand the source roles <Icon name="right-arrow" /></span>
    </a>
    <ol class="product-lane">
      <li><a href="/arcantry/getting-started/"><Icon name="approve-check-circle" /><span><strong>Inspect a project safely</strong><small>Get the source map without creating or rewriting project files</small></span><Icon name="right-arrow" /></a></li>
      <li><a href="/arcantry/adoption/#choose-an-adoption-path"><Icon name="code-branch" /><span><strong>Choose the right footprint</strong><small>Compare zero-footprint, external, tracked, selective, and full integration</small></span><Icon name="right-arrow" /></a></li>
      <li><a href="/arcantry/guides/todo-txt/"><Icon name="document" /><span><strong>Make queues reviewable</strong><small>Preview shared or private todo.txt changes before writing them</small></span><Icon name="right-arrow" /></a></li>
      <li><a href="/arcantry/skills/#choose-a-distribution-path"><Icon name="puzzle" /><span><strong>Give an agent one procedure</strong><small>Link one focused workflow or use the complete versioned catalog</small></span><Icon name="right-arrow" /></a></li>
    </ol>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="journey" id="quickstart" aria-labelledby="quickstart-title">
  <div class="journey-heading">
    <p>Adoption strategies</p>
    <div>
      <h2 id="quickstart-title">Prefer complete integration, keep selective use available</h2>
      <p>Arcantry is most valuable when it can describe the complete project knowledge stack. That is the recommended destination for both new and existing projects, but every source and capability remains independently adoptable.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <ol class="journey-steps">
    <li>
      <span class="step-icon"><Icon name="padlock" /></span>
      <div>
        <span class="step-number">01</span>
        <span class="step-label">Recommended when the repository is off limits</span>
        <h3>Keep adoption private</h3>
        <p>Use global tools and private project context when you cannot change how an established repository is managed.</p>
        <strong class="step-outcome">Benefit: project-aware tools with no repository changes.</strong>
        <a href="/arcantry/adoption/#external-configuration">Keep the repository untouched <Icon name="right-arrow" /></a>
      </div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <span class="step-icon"><Icon name="puzzle" /></span>
      <div>
        <span class="step-number">02</span>
        <span class="step-label">Supported when full adoption is not practical</span>
        <h3>Connect selected capabilities</h3>
        <p>Use only the queues, skills, requirements, or release history that help now, with independent scopes and responsibilities.</p>
        <strong class="step-outcome">Benefit: immediate value without forcing the complete model.</strong>
        <a href="/arcantry/adoption/#queues-or-skills-only">See selective paths <Icon name="right-arrow" /></a>
      </div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <span class="step-icon"><Icon name="star" /></span>
      <div>
        <span class="step-number">03</span>
        <span class="step-label">Recommended for new and existing projects</span>
        <h3>Adopt the complete model</h3>
        <p>Connect authority, release history, queues, skills, settings, privacy, and validation boundaries as one coherent system.</p>
        <strong class="step-outcome">Benefit: every tool starts from the same explicit project contract.</strong>
        <a href="/arcantry/adoption/#tracked-configuration">Plan complete integration <Icon name="right-arrow" /></a>
      </div>
    </li>
  </ol>
</section>

<ArcantryKnowledgeGraph />
